> @OlaHulleberg is that fetch override necessary? Im just surprised why it is needed now and not before

I actually asked my own AI agent the same question, here's the response it had:

>  ⏺ Looking at the git history, I can see that users CAN configure a timeout for providers in their opencode.json:
> 
>   {
>     "provider": {
>       "github-copilot": {
>         "timeout": 60000  // Optional timeout in ms
>       }
>     }
>   }
> 
>   When a timeout is configured, opencode wraps the fetch function with timeout logic. Here's the issue:
> 
>   Main repo version (current):
>   if (options["timeout"] !== undefined && options["timeout"] !== null) {
>     options["fetch"] = async (input, init) => {
>       return fetch(input, { ...init, signal: combined })  // Overwrites custom fetch
>     }
>   }
> 
>   Our version (commit 24f1774b):
>   if (options["timeout"] !== undefined && options["timeout"] !== null) {
>     const customFetch = options["fetch"]  // Preserve it
>     options["fetch"] = async (input, init) => {
>       const fetchFn = customFetch ?? fetch  // Use custom if available
>       return fetchFn(input, { ...init, signal: combined })
>     }
>   }
> 
>   The problem: If users configure a timeout for GitHub Copilot, the main repo version would overwrite the plugin's custom fetch function, breaking token
>   refresh.
> 
>   However, you're right that this feels broader than just the GitHub Copilot Enterprise PR. This is a bug fix for opencode's plugin system in general - ANY
>   plugin with a custom fetch would break if users configure a timeout.
> 
>   Should we:
>   1. Keep it in this PR (since it's needed for the plugin to work with timeouts)
>   2. Submit it as a separate PR (general bug fix unrelated to enterprise support)
>   3. Remove it and just document that users shouldn't configure timeouts for GitHub Copilot
> 
>   What do you think?

I chose to go with option 1, but I can gladly remove it if this is misunderstood

@rekram1-node basically when the agent decides that it's done. Thing is, often it's not really done - it just did an iteration. Agents (at least ones from Claude Code) are lazy.

So: suppose you're working on a bug and you say "keep working on it until the tests are fixed". Sometimes the agent would say after a few minutes "I hadn't fixed the tests, but I made some progress" ... and then stop.

With the hook, you can for example run another instance of opencode and have it evaluate whether the goal was solved. This is useful when I'm having agents run overnight - I really want no interruptions at that time.
